:li {} [:a {:href "/statistics/medal/country"} "Medals by country"]] "\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/statistics/medal/athlete"} "Medals by athlete"]] "\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/statistics/medal_table"} "Medal tables"]] "\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/statistics/participation"} "Participations"]] "\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/records"} "Olympic Records"]] "\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/statistics/age"} "Age records"]] "\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/statistics/age_at_death"} "Age at death"]] "\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/lists"} "Lists"]] "\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/today"} "Today"]] "\n" [:li {}] "\n" [:li {:class "divider"}] "\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/statistics"} "Statistics"]] "\n"] "\n"] "\n\n\n\n\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/feedbacks/new"} "Feedback"]] "\n" [:form {:class "navbar-form navbar-left", :role "search", :action "/athletes/quick_search", :accept-charset "UTF-8", :method "post"} [:input {:name "utf8", :type "hidden", :value "✓", :autocomplete "off"}] [:input {:type "hidden", :name "authenticity_token", :value "rwIQQzc4ku2_9dSUduogGV8nEM7mktp1g0EdzHE9s9jWXvTySDtK4Dh2pBDEzkJ_NQFG2d9yMzxu7l3Gohb94Q", :autocomplete "off"}] [:div {:class "form-group"} "\n" [:input {:type "text", :name "query", :id "query", :class "form-control", :placeholder "Athlete search"}] "\n"] "\n" [:input {:type "submit", :name "commit", :value "Go", :class "btn btn-default", :data-disable-with "Go"}] "\n"] "\n"] "\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:div {:class "container"} "\n" [:ol {:class "breadcrumb"} "\n" [:li {} "\n" [:a {:href "/"} "Home"] "\n"] "\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/editions"} "Games"]] [:li {:class "active"} "1928 Winter Olympics Overview"] "\n"] "\n\n\n" [:h1 {} "1928 Winter Olympics"] "\n" [:h2 {} "Facts"] "\n" [:table {:class "biotable"} "\n" [:tbody {} [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Competition type"] "\n" [:td {} "Olympic Games"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Number and Year"] "\n" [:td {} "II / 1928"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Host city"] "\n" [:td {} "\nSankt Moritz, Switzerland\n(" [:a {:href "/venues/editions/30"} "Venues"] ")\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Opening ceremony"] "\n" [:td {} "\n11 February\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Closing ceremony"] "\n" [:td {} "\n17 February\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Competition dates"] "\n" [:td {} "\n11 – 19 February\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Participants"] "\n" [:td {} "\n" [:a {:href "/counts/edition/30"} "458"] "\nfrom " [:a {:href "/editions/30/countries"} "25 countries"] "\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Medal events"] "\n" [:td {} "\n14\nin 8 disciplines\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Other events"] "\n" [:td {} "\n2\nin 2 disciplines\n"] "\n"] "\n"]] "\n\n" [:h2 {} "\nOverview\n\n"] "\n" [:div {:class "description"} "\n" [:p {} "The Swiss were well-prepared to bring the Olympic Winter Games to the famous winter resort of St. Moritz in 1928.  But there was one political cloud that confronted the organizers.  Since World War I, Germany had not been invited to the Olympic Games, but the " [:a {:href "/organizations/1"} "IOC"] " felt the 1928 St. Moritz Games would be the right time for Germany to re-enter the Olympic arena.  However, this was still greeted with hostility by the other nations, and the Belgian Olympic Committee actually protested German admission to the 1928 Olympic Winter Games.  These would also be the first Olympic Games at which IOC President " [:a {:href "/athletes/899082"} "Henri de Baillet-Latour"] " would preside, having replaced " [:a {:href "/athletes/920613"} "Baron de Coubertin"] " in 1925."] "\n\n" [:p {} "With St. Moritz having had bobsled and skeleton runs for years, and with multiple skating rinks and lakes available and the nearby mountains for skiing, the village needed to do minimal building in preparation for the Games.  But the Swiss did construct the world’s highest ski jump especially for the 1928 Olympic Winter Games."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The 1928 Olympic Winter Games were highly successful, but the organizers had to contend with poor weather.  The “Föhn”, a strong wind coming down the leeward side of a mountain and carrying warm weather with it, postponed several events and forced the cancellation of one.  On the morning of the " [:a {:href "/results/1892"} "50 kilometre cross-country skiing"] ", the temperature was about 0° C (32° F) but, during the competition, the “Föhn” came in and temperatures rose to 25° C (77° F) in mid-day, playing havoc with the snow and waxing conditions."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Later that night, the warm weather brought rain that poured down and ruined the ski courses.  Fortunately, snow and frost over the next few days rescued them.  But the “Föhn” affected several other events as well.  In the " [:a {:href "/results/964"} "10,000 metre speed skating"] ", the United States’ " [:a {:href "/athletes/87432"} "Irving Jaffee"] " had the best time after the first few runs, as the “Föhn” was bringing in warm weather and melting the rink.  The event was halted because of the conditions and it was never restarted.  Jaffee is listed by some American sources, incorrectly, as having won the event, but it actually was never contested to a conclusion.  The " [:a {:href "/results/236"} "5-man bob race"] " also brought the wrath of the “Föhn”, when the bob course was thawed and the four-run contest was shortened to only two runs."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The individual stars of the Games were " [:a {:href "/athletes/98260"} "Clas Thunberg"] ", who won two more golds in speed skating; " [:a {:href "/athletes/86270"} "Johan Grøttumsbraaten"] ", who won two nordic skiing golds; " [:a {:href "/athletes/86175"} "Gillis Grafström"] ", who won his third consecutive " [:a {:href "/results/12654"} "figure skating title"] "; and " [:a {:href "/athletes/81280"} "Sonja Henie"] ", who " [:a {:href "/results/12678"} "won her first"] " of three Olympic figure skating championships.  But perhaps the real star of the 1928 Winter Olympics was the Canadian " [:a {:href "/results/20038"} "ice hockey"] " leviathan that, in the absence of American participation, was unchallenged in winning the title, with a goal margin of 38-0."] "\n\n"] "\n\n" [:h2 {} "Bid process"] "\n" [:div {:class "description"} "\n" [:p {} "Out of three Swiss cities, St. Moritz was selected at the 25th " [:a {:href "/meetings"} "IOC Session"] " in Lisboa, on 6 May 1926, by a vote of 22 in favor with one abstention. The other bidding cities were Davos and Engelberg."] "\n\n"] "\n\n" [:h2 {} "Ceremonies"] "\n" [:table {:class "table table-striped table-condensed"} "\n" [:tbody {} [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} [:a {:href "/ceremonies/officially_opened"} "Officially opened by"]] "\n" [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/1100027"} "Edmund Schulthess"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/SUI"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/SUI.png"}] "SUI"]] [:td {}] [:td {} "President"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} [:a {:href "/ceremonies/athlete_oath"} "Taker of the Athlete's Oath"]] "\n" [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/85437"} "Hans Eidenbenz"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/SUI"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/SUI.png"}] "SUI"]] [:td {} "Nordic Combined"] [:td {}] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} [:a {:href "/ceremonies"} "Flagbearers"]] "\n" [:td {} [:a {:href "/flagbearers?edition_id=30"} "Full list"]] "\n" [:td {}] "\n" [:td {}] "\n" [:td {}] "\n"] "\n"]] "\n\n" [:h2 {} "Medal Disciplines"] "\n" [:table {:class "table table-striped"} "\n" [:tbody {} [:tr {} "\n" [:td {} "\n" [:a {:href "/editions/30/sports/BOB"} "Bobsleigh"] "\n"] "\n" [:td {} "\n" [:a {:href "/editions/30/sports/IHO"} "Ice Hockey"] "\n"] "\n" [:td {} "\n" [:a {:href "/editions/30/sports/SJP"} "Ski Jumping"] "\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:td {} "\n" [:a {:href "/editions/30/sports/CCS"} "Cross Country Skiing"] "\n"] "\n" [:td {} "\n" [:a {:href "/editions/30/sports/NCB"} "Nordic Combined"] "\n"] "\n" [:td {} "\n" [:a 